{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"cd /home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/runs/2026-10-07_16-54-34-swiftpaxos_epaxos-real-run/draft && /home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/.venv/bin/python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nsrcs=[{\\\"id\\\":\\\"S40-client-command-allocation\\\",\\\"file\\\":\\\"client/buffer.go\\\",\\\"start_line\\\":96,\\\"end_line\\\":100,\\\"kind\\\":\\\"code_observation\\\"},\n {\\\"id\\\":\\\"S41-client-write-size\\\",\\\"file\\\":\\\"client/client.go\\\",\\\"start_line\\\":198,\\\"end_line\\\":214,\\\"kind\\\":\\\"code_observation\\\"}]\nfb={\\\"ref_ids\\\":[\\\"4cd03e5aa3ec4080807408f6b3678c14\\\",\\\"3ee262e5afd44c419b561c23376ffafb\\\",\\\"S40-client-command-allocation\\\",\\\"S41-client-write-size\\\",\\\"S38-client-command-size\\\"],\n \\\"answered\\\":\\\"The client-side half of the codec finding's applicability is answered: the client caps nothing. Its loop allocates one buffer of exactly the configured command size (make([]byte, c.psize) with psize taken from config.CommandSize) and fills it with random bytes, and every write sends that whole buffer as the command value, with no length check anywhere on the path. So the applicability of claim C10 is not hypothetical: a deployment that configures values larger than the 65535-byte length field makes every write an oversized command, which the three confirmed scenarios show ends in a truncated value, a corrupted following command, and a receiver-side decode panic. The shipped aws.conf's 1000-byte setting is what keeps the default deployment inside the field's range.\\\",\n \\\"remaining\\\":[\\\"whether any configuration in or outside the repository uses a command size above the field's range, which is a deployment question rather than a code one\\\",\n   \\\"what a participant does with a stream displaced by the leftover bytes (still unread)\\\",\n   \\\"the two paused candidates' resume conditions and the structurally stuck scope issue\\\"],\n \\\"rationale\\\":\\\"The read answers the last applicability question of a confirmed finding with two sources and materially raises how the finding should be read: the oversized value is produced by the client's own configuration, not only by a hypothetical caller. No new claim is needed because the applicability already names a configuration-driven size; the sources are recorded so a reviewer can see it is enforced by the implementation.\\\",\n \\\"understanding\\\":\\\"updated\\\",\\\"question_updates\\\":{}}\nsub={\\\"action\\\":\\\"research\\\",\\\"rationale\\\":\\\"Records the sources that answer whether the client caps the command size it builds: it allocates exactly the configured size and sends it uncapped, so a deployment with a value above the 65535-byte field turns every write into the codec failure the C10 scenarios describe. Two client ranges are registered; no map object changes.\\\",\n \\\"sources\\\":srcs,\\\"feedback\\\":fb}\njson.dump(sub,open('submission-client-cap.json','w'),indent=2,ensure_ascii=False)\nprint('ok')\nPY\ncd .. && /home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/.venv/bin/python -I -B -c \\\"import sys; sys.path.insert(0, '/home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/src'); from consensus_assurance.cli import main; raise SystemExit(main())\\\" validate --run /home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/runs/2026-10-07_16-54-34-swiftpaxos_epaxos-real-run --submission submission-client-cap.json 2>&1 | head -8\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
