{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"submission\": \"submission-client-cap.json\", \"summary\": \"Turn 75 answered the last applicability question of the codec finding and materially sharpened how it should be read.\\n\\nWhat was read. The client's loop allocates exactly one buffer of the configured command size — make([]byte, c.psize), with psize coming from the configuration's CommandSize — fills it with random bytes, and every write sends that whole buffer as the command value, with no length check anywhere on the path. So the client caps nothing: a deployment configured with values larger than the 65535-byte length field makes every write an oversized command, and the three confirmed C10 scenarios then apply in sequence — a truncated value, a shifted following command, and a receiver-side decode panic that the reader has no recover for. The shipped aws.conf's 1000-byte setting is the only thing keeping the default deployment inside the field's range.\\n\\nHow this changes the reading. The C10 claim already names a configuration-driven command size, but until now the oversized value was only a *constructible* caller choice. With the client's own allocation in hand, the applicability is enforced by the implementation itself: the value is exactly what the operator configures, so the finding's importance is bounded by a deployment setting rather than by a hypothetical caller. No new claim is needed for that — the claim's applicability already covers it — so the two client ranges are registered as sources and the answer is recorded as feedback, with the deployment question (whether any real configuration uses such a size) left as the remaining unknown.\\n\\nState. Audit spec moved to version 16; the eleven settled claim families, the two paused candidates, the structurally stuck scope issue and the remaining unread items are unchanged, and no requirement, premise or earlier observation was weakened.\"}"}}
